WEBVTT


00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:06.520
k2tog, psso ssk, yo.

00:00:06.690 --> 00:00:12.630
These terms may be mysterious to you, but
as a machine knitter, if you want to use

00:00:12.630 --> 00:00:17.970
hand knitting patterns, it's critical that
you understand what these terms mean and

00:00:17.970 --> 00:00:22.710
you're able to translate them
to machine knitting techniques.

00:00:23.430 --> 00:00:26.730
The concept of deliberately
making holes in your knitting.

00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:33.450
Also known as lace, is the same no matter
whether you're using two sticks or a

00:00:33.450 --> 00:00:34.260
knitting machine.

00:00:36.090 --> 00:00:41.460
We'll talk about three common knitting
techniques and how to work them on the

00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:42.060
machine.

00:00:43.110 --> 00:00:46.980
First, if you're lucky enough to have
a standard gauge machine with a lace

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:50.940
carriage, the lace carriage will
make all the transfers for you.

00:00:51.655 --> 00:00:56.005
But it's important that you understand
what's happening between your patterning

00:00:56.005 --> 00:01:01.975
device, punch card or electronics and the
needles on the needle bed and the lace

00:01:01.975 --> 00:01:02.725
carriage.

00:01:03.805 --> 00:01:08.845
Let's look at how lace works on all
machines, no matter how the needles are

00:01:08.845 --> 00:01:11.125
selected or transferred.

00:01:14.205 --> 00:01:19.544
First is a yarn over, also known
as an eyelet on the machine.

00:01:19.664 --> 00:01:25.035
Anytime you have an empty needle and work
and you knit across the row, the working

00:01:25.035 --> 00:01:27.494
yarn will travel over that needle.

00:01:28.505 --> 00:01:33.244
When you knit the next row, that strand
becomes a stitch and you have an eyelet.

00:01:33.934 --> 00:01:38.255
Now, to empty the needle, you need to
knit two stitches together somehow.

00:01:38.940 --> 00:01:43.200
Have you heard the term right-leaning
decrease in the left leaning decrease?

00:01:43.650 --> 00:01:48.850
This refers to both the position of the
decreased stitch in relation to the yarn

00:01:48.870 --> 00:01:54.750
over and the direction it's leaning
or the direction it was transferred.

00:01:55.290 --> 00:02:01.890
In hand knitting, we refer to knit two
together as a right leaning decrease.

00:02:08.370 --> 00:02:14.100
Slip, slip knit or SSK as
a left leaning decrease.

00:02:17.370 --> 00:02:22.350
When you're hand knitting, these depend
on how you insert your needle into the

00:02:22.350 --> 00:02:26.430
stitches and form the decreased stitch.

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:32.580
Also in hand, that end, you're looking at
your knitting from the right side, public

00:02:32.580 --> 00:02:35.760
side or the knit side of the work.

00:02:36.300 --> 00:02:39.420
So left and right
leaning makes some sense.

00:02:40.350 --> 00:02:45.450
But for machine knitters, the purl side of
the knitting is facing us, so we have to

00:02:45.450 --> 00:02:46.950
think a little backwards.

00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:53.370
Picture this, use a transfer tool
and remove a stitch from its needle.

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:57.240
Transfer that stitch over to its neighbor.

00:02:57.330 --> 00:02:59.170
Either right or left it doesn't matter.

00:02:59.850 --> 00:02:59.940
Okay.

00:03:00.420 --> 00:03:03.720
Look closely and the
stitch you transferred.

00:03:04.050 --> 00:03:06.000
Remember we worked from the purl side.

00:03:06.150 --> 00:03:09.750
That stitch is hiding behind
the stitch next to it.

00:03:10.830 --> 00:03:13.110
This would be like a yarn over,

00:03:13.140 --> 00:03:17.300
then the two together in hand knitting.

00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:19.894
We'll examine why this
matters in a minute.

00:03:21.005 --> 00:03:24.545
Now, if you're getting a little
overwhelmed, hang in there with me.

00:03:24.635 --> 00:03:29.584
Let's for now, let's take a look at
a two-step machine knitting transfer.

00:03:30.665 --> 00:03:37.894
This time we'll first transfer stitch and
then move both of those stitches to the

00:03:37.894 --> 00:03:43.274
empty needle to reposition
the upcoming yarn over.

00:03:44.945 --> 00:03:50.445
So when you knit the next row and these
two stitches are knit and we look from the

00:03:50.445 --> 00:03:57.465
knit side of the knitting, notice that the
front facing stitch is really defined and

00:03:57.465 --> 00:03:58.605
it's leaning to the right.

00:03:59.805 --> 00:04:06.165
This would be the equivalent of an SSK,
yarn over sequence for hand knitters.

00:04:07.965 --> 00:04:09.495
Now, why does this matter?

00:04:10.745 --> 00:04:15.695
In this sample I used the
simple one-step transfer method.

00:04:16.235 --> 00:04:20.084
First I transferred, right,
and then I transferred left.

00:04:21.795 --> 00:04:27.785
In this sample, I used the two-step
transfer method, and you can see the more

00:04:27.785 --> 00:04:30.094
defined stitch pattern.

00:04:33.525 --> 00:04:36.635
Looking at more complex lace patterns.

00:04:37.275 --> 00:04:40.335
You can see how this
does make a difference.

00:04:45.705 --> 00:04:51.285
Okay, so it's the direction of the stitch
transfer in relation to the position of

00:04:51.285 --> 00:04:57.585
the eyelet or that yarn over that
determines the appearance of any lace

00:04:57.645 --> 00:04:58.425
sequence.

00:04:59.775 --> 00:05:05.205
The best way to really understand these
concepts and apply them to creating your

00:05:05.205 --> 00:05:11.205
own lace patterns with electronics or
punch cards or even hand manipulation is

00:05:11.205 --> 00:05:12.765
to try them.

00:05:13.365 --> 00:05:14.265
Happy knitting.